# Pitchvilla — Design System Rules

This Flutter app is a screen-by-screen conversion of the Pitchvilla React
Native app. The design system was built once, deliberately, to mirror
`frontend/src/theme.ts` and `design_guidelines.json` from the original RN
project exactly. **Treat it as frozen.** Every visual value in this app
already has a home in `lib/theme/`. Before writing any new color, spacing,
radius, shadow, font, or icon value, look there first.

## The token files (source of truth)

- `lib/theme/colors.dart` → `AppColors` — the only place colors are defined.
- `lib/theme/spacing.dart` → `AppSpacing` (4/8/12/16/24/32/48) and
  `AppRadius` (6/12/20/24/999). **Nothing else lives in this file.**
- `lib/theme/text_styles.dart` → `AppFontWeight` and `AppTextStyles`, all
  built on `kFontFamily` (Poppins).
- `lib/theme/shadows.dart` → `AppShadows`.
- `lib/theme/app_theme.dart` → assembles `ThemeData`, overrides Material's
  entire default `TextTheme` so no fallback font can ever appear.

## Hard rules

1. **Never add a new token class to `lib/theme/`** (no `AppSizes`,
   `AppBorders`, `AppDurations`, or similar) without asking the user first
   and getting explicit sign-off. This has happened before and had to be
   reverted — every value it introduced (`chip`, `control`, `dot`,
   `iconMd`, `thin`/`medium`/`thick`) was just arithmetic on existing
   `AppSpacing` values or a literal, and belonged inline at the call site
   like every other screen in the app already does it.
2. **Font is Poppins, everywhere, no exceptions.** Every `TextStyle` must
   either come from `AppTextStyles.xxx` (optionally `.copyWith(...)`) or
   explicitly set `fontFamily: kFontFamily`. Never leave `fontFamily`
   unset on a literal `TextStyle`, and never reference a system or
   default font.
3. **Icons are `Ionicons` from `package:flutter_vector_icons`, everywhere,
   no exceptions.** Never use Flutter's built-in `Icons.*` (Material
   icons) or `CupertinoIcons`. This mirrors the RN app's actual
   `@expo/vector-icons` usage (Feather/FontAwesome/Ionicons/MaterialIcons
   sets — but only Ionicons ended up used in practice, so that's the
   standard here).
   - If a specific `Ionicons` glyph doesn't exist in the bundled font
     (a known gap — e.g. `sparkles` isn't in this package's Ionicons set),
     substitute the closest existing `Ionicons` glyph and leave a comment
     explaining the substitution. Do **not** switch that one icon to a
     different icon family as a workaround.
   - If `flutter build web` fails on icon tree-shaking (`Codepoint N not
     found in font, aborting`), the fix is the `--no-tree-shake-icons`
     build flag — **not** swapping the icon family. This exact mistake
     happened once already (a shared `BackChevron` widget was switched to
     `Icons.arrow_back_ios_new_rounded` to dodge this) and had to be
     reverted.
4. **Colors only from `AppColors`.** A handful of one-off translucent
   overlay values (e.g. header image tints, dark circular button
   backgrounds) exist as raw `Color(0x......)` literals in a few widgets —
   these are intentional, byte-exact translations of specific `rgba(...)`
   values from the original RN screens, not tokens. If you need a new
   translucent overlay, compute it the same way (match the RN source's
   literal rgba value) rather than approximating or inventing a new shade.
   **Pitchvilla colour roles — the app is white-dominant and yellow is on a
   budget.** Pages, headers, cards and form fields are white / `offWhite` /
   gray with `ink` text. `AppColors.brand` (#FFCC00) may appear ONLY as:
   (a) a primary-CTA fill (`PillVariant.primary`) and the thin stroke on the
   secondary CTA; (b) a solid highlight card for a deliberate feature moment
   (Results top match, Career-DNA final result, Home promo / counseling
   cards); (c) a small signal — 2px selected-state stroke, tab underline,
   focus border, unread dot, progress fill, level node, avatar circle,
   success-check circle, the logo dot. Never a page/header/banner fill, a
   pale wash (there is no tint token), a tag, or a selected-chip fill: a
   solid yellow pill reads as a CTA. Selected chips/tiles are a white fill +
   2px brand stroke + semibold label (`AppChip`); unselected are `offWhite`.
   Utility headers use `PageHeaderBar` (white, hairline divider). Yellow is
   never text or icon colour on white. The bottom-nav active tab is not
   yellow (filled icon + ink semibold label).
5. **Spacing and radius only from the `AppSpacing` / `AppRadius` scales.**
   Don't hardcode a raw pixel number where an existing token fits. A few
   sub-token literals (e.g. `3`, `4` for a progress-bar track or carousel
   dot) exist because they match a specific one-off value in the original
   RN source exactly — that's fine to replicate, but don't generalize a
   new named constant out of them.
6. **Shadows only from `AppShadows`.** Never construct a raw `BoxShadow`
   inline.
7. **Structural changes to the design system itself are a "stop and ask"
   moment.** Adding a new theme file, renaming an existing token, changing
   what `AppTheme.light` returns, or introducing a second font — all of
   these need the user's explicit go-ahead before you touch them. Feature
   work (new screens, new widgets, new state) does not need this — it's
   specifically the shared `lib/theme/` contract that's protected.

## Quick self-check before committing a UI change

- Did I add any class to `lib/theme/` that isn't `AppColors`,
  `AppSpacing`, `AppRadius`, `AppFontWeight`, `AppTextStyles`,
  `AppShadows`, or `AppTheme`? → stop, ask first.
- Does every icon in my diff come from `Ionicons`? → grep your diff for
  `Icons\.` and `CupertinoIcons\.` — both should return nothing.
- Does every `TextStyle` in my diff either use `AppTextStyles` or set
  `fontFamily: kFontFamily`? → grep for `TextStyle(` and check each one.
- Did I hardcode a color, spacing, or radius number that isn't already a
  named token? → prefer the token.
